# Principles

- Shorter is better.
- Never add fallback logic "just in case". Let edge cases surface.
- Refactor boldly. No backward compatibility unless asked.
- Prefer co-location: keep related code close together.
- Fix the root cause where the system first went wrong, and every instance of the same pattern.
- When two fixes sharing one premise fail, test the premise before writing a third.
{{- if eq .agent "codex" }}
- Extract helpers only when called 3+ times and non-obvious.
{{- end }}

<important if="you are the main conversation talking to the user, not a subagent; a subagent skips this section and just does the work it was handed">
- Progressive outlining: on non-trivial prose and code alike, settle the coarse structure (outline, headings, interfaces) with the user first — discuss and gather information at that level; write the actual text or implementation last. Skip this when running unattended.
- Before non-trivial work, define done as checks a machine can run: a passing command or a metric threshold.
- Answer questions you can settle by running something (behavior, output, perf) by running it, not by asking the user.
{{- if ne .agent "zed" }}
- Invoke the **tracking-tasks** skill and read related task notes before your first edit or command, even for a single-line edit.
{{- end }}
</important>

<important if="you are running shell commands">
- Use `trash` instead of `rm`.
- Prefer single quotes for shell arguments to avoid backtick command substitution.
- zsh does not word-split `$VAR`; pass multiple args as an array (`args=(a b); cmd $args`).
- Search with `rg`, or `ast-grep` for structural code search and rewrites; never `grep`.
{{- if eq .agent "claude" }}
- `unsetopt CLOBBER` is active: write to existing files, including fresh `mktemp` ones, with `>|`.
- Set `dangerouslyDisableSandbox` on every command: the sandbox denies writes mid-operation and leaves state half-applied (e.g. a git branch without its worktree).
  - If a command still runs sandboxed, pass `mktemp` a template (`mktemp "$TMPDIR/tmp.XXXXXX"`): without one, macOS `mktemp` ignores `$TMPDIR` and fails.
{{- end }}
</important>

<important if="you are adding or updating dependencies">
- Always use near-latest versions; look the current version up rather than recalling it.
</important>

<important if="you are writing or modifying tests">
- Tests are specifications: test code expresses the specification of the implementation.
- Explicitly mark each section with comments: `// Arrange`, `// Act`, `// Assert`.
- Name tests with a sentence stating the situation, operation, and expected result in that order, e.g. "when a valid token is provided, retrieving user information succeeds".
</important>

<important if="you are about to commit or mark work as done">
- Prove it on the real artifact: run it and read the actual output, not a proxy like "it compiles".
{{- if ne .agent "zed" }}
- Invoke the **no-comments** skill on the changed files.
{{- end }}
- Remove or consolidate temporary TDD tests: keep roughly one focused test per stated behavior, and only where its lasting value justifies the maintenance cost.
</important>

<important if="you are writing text for humans: docs, descriptions, test names, review comments, issue/PR titles and bodies">
- Language: match what the repository already uses for the same kind of text (recent commits, PRs, issues, docs). With no precedent, English if `gh repo view --json visibility -q '.visibility'` is `PUBLIC`, else Japanese.
- In Japanese, leave English only for identifiers, product/library names, API names, and established terms when clearer; no awkward mixed Japanese-English phrasing, and no trailing `。` in description fields and similar prose.
- Every line earns its place. Delete any line where you can't say what the reader loses without it.
- Keep only what's not obvious from the diff or the name; state what something is, not how it's implemented or why it changed.
</important>
{{- if eq .agent "zed" }}

<important if="you are writing a commit message">
- Use Conventional Commit subjects: "type(scope): subject", max 72 chars, in English.
- Valid subject types: fix, feat, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore, revert.
</important>
{{- end }}
